"{\"judgments\":[{\"candidate\":0,\"status\":\"supported\",\"reason\":\"`shouldTrace` returns true whenever `hasSubscribers` is not exactly `false`, and the diff's own comment states that on Node 18 the aggregated TracingChannel getter is `undefined`. On that runtime the guard therefore always passes, so every `client.query` carrying a callback allocates the `query`/`client` context and calls `queryChannel.traceCallback(...)` with no subscribers attached (same for `Client.connect`), which contradicts the change's stated requirement that there be no overhead when nothing subscribes. The proposed fix is viable because `tracingChannel()` exposes `start`/`end`/`asyncStart`/`asyncEnd`/`error` as ordinary channels whose own `hasSubscribers` flags remain usable when the aggregate getter is missing.\",\"evidence\":[\"packages/pg/lib/diagnostics.js: `function shouldTrace(channel) {` / `return channel.hasSubscribers !== false`\",\"packages/pg/lib/diagnostics.js comment: \\\"Check explicitly for `false` rather than truthiness because the aggregated `hasSubscribers` getter on TracingChannel is `undefined` on Node 18 ... When `undefined`, we assume there may be subscribers and trace unconditionally.\\\"\",\"packages/pg/lib/client.js: `if (shouldTrace(queryChannel) && query.callback) { const context = { query: {...}, client: {...} } ... queryChannel.traceCallback((tracedCb) => { query.callback = tracedCb; enqueue() }, 0, context, null, enrichedCb)`\",\"PR description: \\\"without active tracing or subscribers the overhead is non-existent so existing users shouldn't be affected\\\"\"],\"duplicate_of\":null,\"unverified_assumptions\":[]},{\"candidate\":1,\"status\":\"supported\",\"reason\":\"Same guard in the pool helper: `hasSubscribers !== false` evaluates true when the aggregate getter is absent (Node 18, as the file's own comment states), so every `pool.connect` builds the `pool` context object, wraps the callback in `enrichedCb`, and runs `poolConnectChannel.traceCallback(...)` even with zero subscribers, defeating the promised no-subscriber idle path. Checking the individual phase channels, which are ordinary channels, avoids the unconditional tracing.\",\"evidence\":[\"packages/pg-pool/diagnostics.js: `function shouldTrace(channel) {` / `return channel.hasSubscribers !== false`\",\"packages/pg-pool/diagnostics.js comment: \\\"the aggregated `hasSubscribers` getter on TracingChannel is `undefined` on Node 18 ... we assume there may be subscribers and trace unconditionally\\\"\",\"packages/pg-pool/index.js: `if (shouldTrace(poolConnectChannel)) { const context = { pool: { totalCount: this.totalCount, idleCount: this.idleCount, waitingCount: this.waitingCount, maxSize: this.options.max } } ... poolConnectChannel.traceCallback((tracedCb) => { response.callback = tracedCb }, 0, context, null, enrichedCb)`\"],\"duplicate_of\":null,\"unverified_assumptions\":[]},{\"candidate\":2,\"status\":\"rejected\",\"reason\":\"The publish mirrors the pool's existing public release notification emitted at the very same point (`this.emit('release', err, client)`), which already fires for every release, including those that subsequently evict the client; the payload also carries `error: err || undefined`, and eviction is separately reported on `pg:pool:remove`, so subscribers are not misled about a client that \\\"stays\\\" in the pool. The suggested change (publish only on the re-enqueue path) would instead drop the release signal and its error for every evicted client, losing information rather than fixing a defect.\",\"evidence\":[\"packages/pg-pool/index.js: `this.emit('release', err, client)` immediately followed by `if (shouldTrace(poolReleaseChannel)) { poolReleaseChannel.publish({ client: { processID: client.processID }, error: err || undefined }) }`\",\"packages/pg-pool/index.js removal path: `poolRemoveChannel.publish({ client: { processID: client.processID } })` inside `_remove`\",\"packages/pg-pool/index.js: `if (err || this.ending || !client._queryable || client._ending || client._poolUseCount >= this.options.maxUses) { ... return this._remove(client, this._pulseQueue.bind(this)) }`\"],\"duplicate_of\":null,\"unverified_assumptions\":[]},{\"candidate\":3,\"status\":\"unresolved\",\"reason\":\"Whether the inner `testTracing(...)` registrations execute depends on the semantics of `helper.Suite.prototype.test`, which is not part of the supplied source (`packages/pg/test/unit/test-helper.js` is absent). If `Suite.test` delegates to a mocha-style `it`/`test` that snapshots the suite's test list when the suite starts, the nested cases never run and the finding holds; if it collects callbacks into an array that is iterated while it is being appended, the nested tests do run. The supplied code establishes only that registration happens inside another test body, not that the inner tests are skipped.\",\"evidence\":[\"packages/pg/test/unit/client/diagnostics-tests.js: `const suite = new helper.Suite()` / `const test = suite.test.bind(suite)` / `const testTracing = (name, cb) => test(name, hasStableTracingChannel ? cb : undefined)`\",\"packages/pg/test/unit/client/diagnostics-tests.js: `testTracing('query diagnostics channel', function () {` / `testTracing('publishes start and asyncEnd on successful query', function (done) {`\"],\"duplicate_of\":null,\"unverified_assumptions\":[\"helper.Suite.test registers with a runner that cannot accept tests registered during another test's execution (test-helper.js is not supplied).\"]},{\"candidate\":4,\"status\":\"unresolved\",\"reason\":\"Same missing context as the query block: the connection case is registered inside the body of the outer `testTracing('connection diagnostics channel', ...)` test, so whether it runs at all is determined by the unsupplied `helper.Suite.test` implementation (mocha-style late registration vs. an append-and-iterate collector). The supplied diff alone does not establish that the inner test is skipped or reordered.\",\"evidence\":[\"packages/pg/test/unit/client/diagnostics-tests.js: `testTracing('connection diagnostics channel', function () {` / `testTracing('publishes start on connect with callback', function (done) {`\",\"packages/pg/test/unit/client/diagnostics-tests.js: `const testTracing = (name, cb) => test(name, hasStableTracingChannel ? cb : undefined)`\"],\"duplicate_of\":null,\"unverified_assumptions\":[\"helper.Suite.test cannot register runnable tests from inside a running test (test-helper.js is not supplied).\"]},{\"candidate\":5,\"status\":\"supported\",\"reason\":\"Same defect as candidate 0 in the same file and line: `channel.hasSubscribers !== false` yields true on a runtime where the aggregate getter is `undefined`, so all `pg:query`/`pg:connection` calls build context and go through `traceCallback` with no subscribers, violating the stated no-overhead-without-subscribers behavior. The suggested variant (`typeof channel.hasSubscribers === 'boolean'` plus per-phase fallback) is one concrete change that fixes the same condition addressed by candidate 0.\",\"evidence\":[\"packages/pg/lib/diagnostics.js: `function shouldTrace(channel) { return channel.hasSubscribers !== false }`\",\"packages/pg/lib/client.js: `if (shouldTrace(connectionChannel)) { ... connectionChannel.traceCallback((tracedCb) => this._connect(tracedCb), 0, context, null, callback) }`\",\"packages/pg/lib/diagnostics.js comment: \\\"the aggregated `hasSubscribers` getter on TracingChannel is `undefined` on Node 18\\\"\"],\"duplicate_of\":0,\"unverified_assumptions\":[]},{\"candidate\":6,\"status\":\"supported\",\"reason\":\"Same defect as candidate 1 in the same file and line: on the runtime where the aggregate `hasSubscribers` getter is missing, `undefined !== false` makes `shouldTrace` true, so pool checkout always wraps the callback and calls `poolConnectChannel.traceCallback`. The proposed boolean-type check with per-phase fallback is the same fix as candidate 1.\",\"evidence\":[\"packages/pg-pool/diagnostics.js: `function shouldTrace(channel) { return channel.hasSubscribers !== false }`\",\"packages/pg-pool/index.js: `if (shouldTrace(poolConnectChannel)) { ... poolConnectChannel.traceCallback(...) }`\"],\"duplicate_of\":1,\"unverified_assumptions\":[]},{\"candidate\":7,\"status\":\"supported\",\"reason\":\"The tracing branch is gated on `query.callback`, so query objects that legitimately have no callback are never traced. In `Client.query`, when `config.submit` is a function and neither `values` nor `callback` is a function, `query.callback` stays unset (`if (!query.callback) { ... }` assigns nothing), so the `else { enqueue() }` path runs and no `pg:query` start/asyncEnd/error events are published. A supported path in this repo exercises exactly that: `Cursor` has `submit(connection)` and no `callback` property, so `client.query(new Cursor(...))` (and event-emitter style `Query` usage) produces no `pg:query` lifecycle events for instrumentations.\",\"evidence\":[\"packages/pg/lib/client.js: `if (shouldTrace(queryChannel) && query.callback) { ... } else { enqueue() }`\",\"packages/pg/lib/client.js: `} else if (typeof config.submit === 'function') { ... if (!query.callback) { if (typeof values === 'function') { query.callback = values } else if (callback) { query.callback = callback } } }`\",\"packages/pg-cursor/index.js: `submit(connection) { this.state = 'submitted' ... }` (no `callback` property is ever assigned on the Cursor instance)\"],\"duplicate_of\":null,\"unverified_assumptions\":[]},{\"candidate\":8,\"status\":\"unresolved\",\"reason\":\"The finding itself is hedged on the unsupplied helper (\\\"If `helper.Suite.test` behaves like normal test registration\\\"). The diff shows only that `testTracing` is invoked from inside an outer test body; whether those registrations are lost, run in order, or run later depends on `packages/pg/test/unit/test-helper.js`, which is not in the supplied source, so the claimed CI-misses-the-test failure cannot be confirmed or refuted here.\",\"evidence\":[\"packages/pg/test/unit/client/diagnostics-tests.js: `testTracing('query diagnostics channel', function () {`\",\"packages/pg/test/unit/client/diagnostics-tests.js: `const suite = new helper.Suite()` / `const test = suite.test.bind(suite)`\"],\"duplicate_of\":null,\"unverified_assumptions\":[\"helper.Suite.test uses a runner whose test list is fixed before the outer test executes.\"]},{\"candidate\":9,\"status\":\"unresolved\",\"reason\":\"Same as candidate 4: the connection diagnostics case is registered from inside another test body, and whether it executes depends on the semantics of the unsupplied `helper.Suite.test`; the supplied diff cannot establish that the case is silently skipped or run out of order.\",\"evidence\":[\"packages/pg/test/unit/client/diagnostics-tests.js: `testTracing('connection diagnostics channel', function () {`\",\"packages/pg/test/unit/client/diagnostics-tests.js: `testTracing('publishes start on connect with callback', function (done) {`\"],\"duplicate_of\":null,\"unverified_assumptions\":[\"helper.Suite.test cannot register runnable tests from within a running test.\"]},{\"candidate\":10,\"status\":\"supported\",\"reason\":\"Same defect as candidate 0 for the same `shouldTrace` helper: when the aggregate `hasSubscribers` getter is absent (Node 18, per the file's comment), the function returns true and every query/connect goes through `traceCallback` with no listeners, contradicting the description's zero-overhead claim. The finding names the same code, same failing condition (unconditional tracing) and the same minimal fix direction (real per-phase subscriber check) as candidate 0.\",\"evidence\":[\"packages/pg/lib/diagnostics.js: `function shouldTrace(channel) { return channel.hasSubscribers !== false }`\",\"PR description: \\\"without active tracing or subscribers the overhead is non-existent\\\"\",\"packages/pg/lib/client.js: `if (shouldTrace(queryChannel) && query.callback) { ... queryChannel.traceCallback(...) }`\"],